research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/fs-limit-exists-implies-bounded-on-the-domain.md-1496-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/fs-limit-exists-implies-bounded-on-the-domain.md-1497-- This is where [[lem-finite-set-has-max]] does its main work on this page: the first $K$ terms are bounded because a nonempty finite set of reals has a maximum, and that fact is proved once, by induction, rather than re-derived here. It is not the only consumer, and no claim of uniqueness is made: [[lem-null-times-bounded]] and [[rem-sequence-conventions]] cite the same lemma, each for a maximum of finitely many reals.
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/fs-limit-exists-implies-bounded-on-the-domain.md-1498-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/fs-limit-exists-implies-bounded-on-the-domain.md-1499-- The converse is false. A bounded sequence need not converge ([[fs-bounded-implies-convergent]]). What is true in that direction is the Bolzano-Weierstrass theorem, which requires a passage to a subsequence; it is **not available at this point in the reading order**, being the subject of the next page of this track, *Monotone Sequences, Bolzano-Weierstrass, and Cauchy Completeness*, where it is proved.
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/fs-limit-exists-implies-bounded-on-the-domain.md-1500-
research/audit/wave7-refuter-tasks/fs-limit-exists-implies-bounded-on-the-domain.md-1501-- Boundedness is exactly what makes [[lem-null-times-bounded]] applicable to a convergent factor, and that is how boundedness enters the product rule of [[thm-algebra-of-limits]].
