research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-final-ex-x-times-psi-tends-to-zero.prompt.md-1990-2.3 **The numerator on the smaller domain.** Assume $M \ne 0$. Since $A_0 \subseteq A$ and $c$ is a limit point of $A_0$ by step 1.4, [L7] gives that the limit of $f|_{A_0}$ at $c$ exists and equals $L$. [step 1.4, L7]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-final-ex-x-times-psi-tends-to-zero.prompt.md-1991-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-a6-final-ex-x-times-psi-tends-to-zero.prompt.md-1992-3.1 **Quotient.** Assume $M \ne 0$. On the domain $A_0$, which has $c$ as a limit point, the two functions $f|_{A_0}$ and $(1/g)|_{A_0}$ have limits $L$ and $1/M$ at $c$ by steps 2.3 and 2.2, and their product is $(f/g)|_{A_0}$ by the field identities; so claim 3, applied on the domain $A_0$, gives that the limit of $(f/g)|_{A_0}$ at $c$ exists and equals $L \cdot (1/M) = L/M$. [step 2.1, step 2.2, step 2.3, L2, L4]
