research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-limit-at-infinity-of-a-rational-function.prompt.md-2010-2.3 **The numerator on the smaller domain.** Assume $M \ne 0$. Since $A_0 \subseteq A$ and $c$ is a limit point of $A_0$ by step 1.4, [L7] gives that the limit of $f|_{A_0}$ at $c$ exists and equals $L$. [step 1.4, L7]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-limit-at-infinity-of-a-rational-function.prompt.md-2011-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-limit-at-infinity-of-a-rational-function.prompt.md-2012-3.1 **Quotient.** Assume $M \ne 0$. On the domain $A_0$, which has $c$ as a limit point, the two functions $f|_{A_0}$ and $(1/g)|_{A_0}$ have limits $L$ and $1/M$ at $c$ by steps 2.3 and 2.2, and their product is $(f/g)|_{A_0}$ by the field identities; so claim 3, applied on the domain $A_0$, gives that the limit of $(f/g)|_{A_0}$ at $c$ exists and equals $L \cdot (1/M) = L/M$. [step 2.1, step 2.2, step 2.3, L2, L4]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-limit-at-infinity-of-a-rational-function.prompt.md-2013-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-refuter-ex-limit-at-infinity-of-a-rational-function.prompt.md-2014-4.1 Claims 1 to 4 are proved, each directly from the $\varepsilon$-$\delta$ definition and none of them through a sequence. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 3.1] ∎
